    "evidence_text": "Characterization of prostaglandin E2 production by Candida albicans.\n\nBACKGROUND Platelet deposition and aggregation are central to the pathogenesis of ischemic complications of acute coronary syndromes (ACS).\n\nProstaglandin E2 promotes intestinal tumor growth via DNA methylation\n\nThese findings uncover a previously unrecognized role for PGE(2) in the promotion of tumor progression.\n\nMacrophages produce a large amount of PGE(2) during inflammation.\n\nThis lipid mediator modulates various immune responses.\n\nPGE(2) acts on macrophages and inhibits production of cytokines such as TNF-alpha and IL-12.\n\nWe generated mPGES-deficient mice and investigated the role of mPGES in vivo.\n\nThe mice showed no augmentation of the PGE(2) production in response to LPS.\n\nPGE(2) is produced within human airways, and both pro- and anti-inflammatory activities have been reported.\n\n27%.\n\n19%.\n\nFinally, treatment of (32)P-labeled whole platelets with prostacyclin resulted in a 90 +/-\n\nProstaglandin E2 as an inhibitory modulator of fibrogenesis in human lung allografts.",
